---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const weeks = [
  {
    week: "1",
    topic: "What numerical analysis is for",
    reading: "Chapter 1: Numerical Analysis, efficiency, data types, elementary problems",
    classroom: "Start with a problem that has an exact mathematical answer and ask why we might still want an approximation. Then compare two implementations that compute the same thing differently.",
    lab: null,
    assignment: "Compare naive and improved implementations for summation or polynomial evaluation. Record when the answers begin to separate."
  },
  {
    week: "2",
    topic: "Error is part of the computation",
    reading: "Chapter 2: Accuracy, precision, floating point, roundoff, loss of significance, stability",
    classroom: "Make floating-point arithmetic visibly disagree with real arithmetic. Do not rescue the example too quickly.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/finite-differences/", label: "Finite differences" },
    assignment: "Find a calculation that becomes worse when a numerical parameter is made smaller. Explain the mechanism."
  },
  {
    week: "3",
    topic: "Linear systems and elimination",
    reading: "Chapter 3.1–3.2: Vectors, matrices, row operations, Gaussian elimination, tridiagonal systems",
    classroom: "Work one elimination far enough by hand that the row operations are concrete, then read the R implementation as the same sequence.",
    lab: null,
    assignment: "Solve one system by row reduction, then verify it with R. Explain any difference between the algorithm and the production function."
  },
  {
    week: "4",
    topic: "Decomposition and iteration",
    reading: "Chapter 3.3–3.5: LU, Cholesky, Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel, least squares",
    classroom: "Put Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel on the same system and ask students to predict which residual falls faster before running either method.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/iterative-linear-algebra/", label: "Jacobi vs. Gauss-Seidel" },
    assignment: "Modify a well-behaved system until one iterative method converges poorly or fails to converge. Diagnose the matrix, not the symptoms."
  },
  {
    week: "5",
    topic: "Interpolation is a choice about what happens between the data",
    reading: "Chapter 4: Polynomial, piecewise, spline, Bézier, multidimensional interpolation",
    classroom: "Use the same data for a global polynomial, piecewise linear interpolation, and a spline. Move one point and watch which parts of each model move with it.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/interpolation/", label: "Interpolation comparison" },
    assignment: "Construct a data set for which the global polynomial behaves badly between otherwise ordinary points."
  },
  {
    week: "6",
    topic: "Differentiation without a symbolic derivative",
    reading: "Chapter 5.1: Finite differences and second derivatives",
    classroom: "Begin with a secant line and shrink h. Ask when the secant should become the tangent, then let floating point complicate the answer.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/finite-differences/", label: "Finite differences" },
    assignment: "For a function with a known derivative, sweep h across several orders of magnitude and explain the error curve."
  },
  {
    week: "7",
    topic: "Newton-Cotes integration",
    reading: "Chapter 5.2: Multipanel rules and Newton-Cotes error",
    classroom: "Draw the geometry of midpoint, trapezoid, and Simpson rules before comparing their numerical results.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/simpson/", label: "Simpson's rule" },
    assignment: "Choose an integrand where increasing the panel count matters a great deal, and another where it hardly matters at all."
  },
  {
    week: "8",
    topic: "Integration without uniform panels",
    reading: "Chapter 5.3–5.4: Gaussian quadrature, adaptive integration, Romberg, Monte Carlo",
    classroom: "Put Gaussian quadrature and Monte Carlo next to each other. One carefully chooses points; the other chooses them randomly. Both are trying to spend function evaluations intelligently.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/gaussian-quadrature/", label: "Gaussian quadrature" },
    lab2: { href: "/methods/monte-carlo/", label: "Monte Carlo" },
    assignment: "Use the same smooth integrand with Simpson, Gauss-Legendre, and Monte Carlo. Compare error against the number of function evaluations."
  },
  {
    week: "9",
    topic: "Three ways to find a root",
    reading: "Chapter 6.1: Bisection, Newton, secant",
    classroom: "Ask what information each method requires before discussing speed. A method that cannot legally start does not get credit for theoretical convergence.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/root-finding/", label: "Root-finder comparison" },
    assignment: "Find one function where Newton is dramatically faster than bisection and another where the starting value makes Newton embarrassing."
  },
  {
    week: "10",
    topic: "Optimization in one dimension",
    reading: "Chapter 6.2: Golden-section search and gradient descent",
    classroom: "Compare interval reduction with slope following. They solve related problems but ask the function for different information.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/golden-section/", label: "Golden-section search" },
    assignment: "Use a multimodal function to show why an interval or starting point can decide which extremum we find."
  },
  {
    week: "11",
    topic: "Optimization in more than one dimension",
    reading: "Chapter 6.3–6.4: Multidimensional gradient descent, hill climbing, simulated annealing, applications",
    classroom: "Put gradient descent and simulated annealing on the same landscape. One follows local slope; the other is sometimes permitted to make a worse move.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/gradient-descent/", label: "Gradient descent" },
    lab2: { href: "/methods/simulated-annealing/", label: "Simulated annealing" },
    assignment: "Change only the starting point or random seed and explain why the final answers differ."
  },
  {
    week: "12",
    topic: "Initial value problems",
    reading: "Chapter 7.1: Euler, Runge-Kutta, linear multistep methods",
    classroom: "Advance one Euler step by hand, then unpack the four slopes in RK4. The difference between the methods should be visible before it is numerical.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/runge-kutta/", label: "Euler, midpoint, and RK4" },
    assignment: "Hold the differential equation fixed and vary h. Compare how the three methods respond."
  },
  {
    week: "13",
    topic: "Systems and boundary value problems",
    reading: "Chapter 7.2: Systems of ODEs and boundary value problems",
    classroom: "Use a coupled system to show that the numerical method has not fundamentally changed; only the state being advanced has.",
    lab: { href: "/workbench/", label: "R Workbench" },
    assignment: "Implement or adapt a small system example and inspect how an error in one component propagates through the rest of the state."
  },
  {
    week: "14",
    topic: "Partial differential equations and numerical stability",
    reading: "Chapter 7.3–7.4: Heat equation, wave equation, applications",
    classroom: "Run a stable discretization first. Then violate the stability condition on purpose. Students should see the numerical solution fail before we ask them to describe why.",
    lab: { href: "/methods/heat-equation/", label: "Heat equation" },
    lab2: { href: "/methods/wave-equation/", label: "Wave equation" },
    assignment: "Construct one stable and one unstable discretization for the same initial condition. Explain the difference in terms of the numerical scheme."
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Fourteen Weeks with CMNA"
  description="A sample fourteen-week course map using the CMNA book, package, and browser laboratories."
>
  <header class="page-head teaching-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Teaching · Course design</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Fourteen weeks with CMNA</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This is one way to turn the book into a semester. It is not a mandate. The
        useful part is the sequence: understand the problem, expose the algorithm,
        run it, and then change something important enough that the result might
        surprise us.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell content">
      <p>
        A traditional numerical analysis course can spend much more time on proofs
        than CMNA does, and there is nothing wrong with that. In that course, this
        map works best as the computational spine running alongside the analytical
        material. In a computational methods or scientific computing course, the
        balance can move farther toward implementation and experiment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Either way, I would resist the temptation to treat the laboratories as
        demonstrations we perform after the lecture. They are more useful when
        students have to make a prediction first. The computer should settle an
        argument, not start one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teaching-schedule-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="teaching-week-list">
        {weeks.map((item) => (
          <article class="teaching-week">
            <div class="teaching-week-number">Week {item.week}</div>
            <div class="teaching-week-main">
              <h2>{item.topic}</h2>
              <p class="teaching-reading"><strong>Reading:</strong> {item.reading}</p>
              <p>{item.classroom}</p>
              {(item.lab || item.lab2) && (
                <p class="teaching-lab-links">
                  <strong>Laboratory:</strong>{" "}
                  {item.lab && <a href={item.lab.href}>{item.lab.label}</a>}
                  {item.lab && item.lab2 && " · "}
                  {item.lab2 && <a href={item.lab2.href}>{item.lab2.label}</a>}
                </p>
              )}
              <p class="teaching-assignment"><strong>Assignment seed:</strong> {item.assignment}</p>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section lab-intro">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <div>
        <p class="section-kicker">Compressing the course</p>
        <h2 class="section-title">Eight weeks is possible. Something has to give.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="content">
        <p>
          For an accelerated course, I would combine Weeks 1 and 2, combine the two
          linear algebra weeks, combine differentiation with Newton-Cotes integration,
          combine root finding with one-dimensional optimization, and treat the final
          two differential-equation weeks as a single unit. Interpolation deserves its
          own week because the distinction between global and local models is too
          useful to rush.
        </p>
        <p>
          The thing I would not compress is the prediction step. If time is short,
          remove an algorithm before removing the opportunity to ask what an algorithm
          should do. Coverage is easy to measure and remarkably easy to overvalue.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
